Ba Tàu.raw:
===Etymology===
{{rfe|vi|
Folk etymology: {{compound|vi|ba|t1=three|tàu|t2=ship}}. Many Chinese [[refugees]] came to Vietnam in boats during the [[Qing]] Dynasty and were known for transporting goods by boat. One possible etymology for {{m|vi|ba||three}} is that the [[Nguyen]] lords permitted the refugees to settle in three communities, in [[Cù Lao Phố]] (present-day [[Đồng Nai]]), the [[Cholon]] district of [[Saigon]], and [[Hà Tiên]].
A more analytical etymology goes here: {{m|vi|ba}} likely arose in the early 19th century as an avoidance form created due to the naming taboo surrounding {{w|lang=vi|Hồ Thị Hoa}} (胡氏華) (see the etymology of {{m|vi|hoa}} for a parallel case). {{m|vi|tàu}} may derive from 曹, which belongs to the same phonetic series as 艚 and can denote “official.” The term {{m|vi|mực Tàu}} is already attested in Alexandre de Rhodes’s Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum, which predates both the immigration of Ming refugees to Cochinchina and the emergence of the folk etymology by nearly three decades. Given that literacy had long been associated with the official class, the sense of {{m|vi|mực Tàu}} could originally have been semantically contaminated into 'ink used by elites for writing' ({{w|lang=en|India ink}}, however, is still in fact an exclusively Chinese invention).}}

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

===Adjective===
{{vi-adj|head=[[ba|Ba]] [[Tàu]]}}

# {{lb|vi|derogatory|sometimes|ethnic slur}} Of or pertaining to ethnic [[Chinese]] people in [[Vietnam]].
# {{lb|vi|derogatory|sometimes|ethnic slur}} [[Chinese]]

