Bà Nà.raw:
{{wp|vi:}}

===Etymology===
{{unc|vi}}.

The French used to name the mountain as {{m|fr|banane|Banane}} because of a lot of banana trees in this mountain. Over time, Vietnamese people started to pronounce ''Banane'' to ''Bà Nà''.

The writer Nguyên Ngọc suggested that the name is derived from a Katu phrase, meaning "my mountain" (some of the documents translate as "my house"). However, Katu-Vietnamese dictionaries define "mountain" as {{m|ktv|kakoong}}, "my" as {{m|ktv||ớng aku}} ("of" as {{m|ktv|ớng}}, "I" as {{m|ktv|aku}}). Both of these words are not pronounced the same as "Bà Nà".

The third proposed etymology is that the name is from the name of the goddess {{w|Thiên Y A Na}} or {{w|Lady Po Nagar}}. "Bà Nà" is a variant of "Po Nagar", but it is unknown why the element ''-gar'' would be dropped. Nowadays, people call the goddess as ''Bà Chúa Ngọc'', some people call her ''Bà Na''. It is unlikely that the tone from ''Na'' to ''Nà'' in the name could be confused.

Another etymology said to be a compound of {{com|vi|bà|alt1=Bà|t1=spirit|alt2=Nà}}. The element {{m|vi|nà}} is attested in ''Đại Nam quấc âm tự vị'' with the meaning "mountain gorge" ({{m|vi|[[vực]] [[núi]]}}) (> {{m|vi|đất nà||land on mountain slope}}). However, the spirits usually use the pronoun {{m|vi|ông|Ông}} (e.g. Ông Hổ, Cá Ông, etc.), not {{m|vi|bà|Bà}}.<ref>[https://www.facebook.com/tiengvietgiaudep/posts/ngu%E1%BB%93n-g%E1%BB%91c-t%C3%AAn-g%E1%BB%8Di-b%C3%A0-n%C3%A0b%C3%A0-n%C3%A0-%C4%91%C6%B0%E1%BB%A3c-bi%E1%BA%BFt-%C4%91%E1%BA%BFn-l%C3%A0-m%E1%BB%99t-%C4%91%E1%BB%8Ba-%C4%91i%E1%BB%83m-du-l%E1%BB%8Bch-n%E1%BB%95i-ti%E1%BA%BFng-%E1%BB%9F-%C4%91/4507162042695335/]</ref>

