Sài Gòn.raw:
{{wp|vi:Thành phố Hồ Chí Minh}}

===Etymology===
{{rfe|vi|Highly likely that this toponym derives from an earlier form of {{bor|vi|km|ព្រៃនគរ}} or {{m|km|ព្រៃគរ}} via regular Vietnamese diachronic sound changes:
* For the first element ''sài'', the onset development from Middle Vietnamese *C-r- > modern /s-/ checks out.
* The lenited /ɣ-/ (< /*-k-/) in ''gòn'' may be explained as resulting from the loss of the Khmer prefixal /nɔ-/, while the final /-n/ can be accounted for by the borrowing of coda /-l/, given that Middle Khmer /-r/ often developed into /-l/ in some modern Khmer dialects (Jacob, 1976).
This also plays into the theme that many Southern Vietnam toponyms come from original Khmer counterparts, such as {{m|vi|Mỹ Tho}}, {{m|vi|Bạc Liêu}}, {{m|vi|Sóc Trăng}}, and {{m|vi|Cà Mau}}, among others.}} 
The original toponym behind ''Sài Gòn'' was attested earliest as {{zh-l|柴棍*}} ([[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: {{m|vi|sài}} {{m|vi|côn}}) in {{w|Lê Quý Đôn}}'s ''{{w|Phủ biên tạp lục}}'' ({{lang|zh|撫邊雜錄}} "Miscellaneous Chronicles of the Pacified Frontier", {{circa2|1776|short=y}}), wherein Lê relates that, in 1674, Cambodian prince {{w|Chey Chettha IV|Ang Sor}} was installed as king over {{lang|zh|[[高綿]]}} (''{{w|Post-Angkor period|Cao Miên}}'') and his brother {{w|Ang Nan}} as vice-king in '''{{lang|zh|柴棍}} (''Sài Gòn'')''' by Vietnamese forces.<ref>Lê Quý Đôn ({{circa2|1776|short=y}}) ''Phủ Biên Tạp Lục'' ({{lang|zh|撫邊雜錄}} lit. 'Miscellaneous Chronicles of the Pacified Frontier'), "Book One", [https://lib.nomfoundation.org/site_media/nom/nlvnpf-0709-01/large/nlvnpf-0709-01-028.jpg folio 28 of 75]</ref>

{{lw|fr|Adrien Launay}}<ref>{{cite-book|fr|author= Adrien Launay|year= 1924|title= Histoire de la Mission de Cochinchine (1688−1823)|chapter= Documents Historiques II: 1728 - 1771|page= 190|pageurl= https://books.google.com/books?id=zZ6AAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA190#v=onepage&q&f=false}}</ref> cites 1747 documents containing the toponyms: ''provincia Rai-gon'' (for ''Sài Gòn'' "Saigon"), ''Rai-gon thong'' (for *''Sài Gòn thượng'' "Upper Saigon"), & ''Rai-gon-ha'' (for *''Sài Gòn hạ'' "Lower Saigon"). 

{{col-top|2|Dismissed folk etymology}}
The proposal that ''Sài Gòn'' is from {{m|zh|堤岸|t=[[embankment]]}} ([[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: {{m|vi|đê ngạn}}) (e.g. by {{w|Francis Garnier}},<ref>Garnier, Francis (1866) "Cholen" in ''Annuaire de la Cochinchine Francaise pour l’année 1866''. pp. 83 - 84. quoted in Vương Hồng Sển (1960) ''Sài Gòn Năm Xưa'', "Phần 2 - 1 - 3 Theo dấu người Tàu, năm 1680 đến miền Nam, năm 1778 lập Đề Ngạn." [https://cvdvn.files.wordpress.com/2018/03/sai-gon-nam-xua.pdf pdf], p. 16 of 125</ref> {{lw|vi|Vương Hồng Sển}},<ref>Vương Hồng Sển (1960) ''Sài Gòn Năm Xưa'', "Phần 4 - 1" & "Phần 4 - 2" [https://cvdvn.files.wordpress.com/2018/03/sai-gon-nam-xua.pdf pdf], p. 34-35 of 125</ref> Cheng Zhenghong,<ref>{{cite-journal|zh|year= 2011|author= 陳正宏|title= 越南漢籍裏的中國代刻本|journal= 歷史文獻|volume= 15|pages= 298-307}}; Vietnamese translation from {{cite-journal|translator= Hoàng Phương Mai|journal= Tạp chí Hán Nôm|date= 31 March 2013|archivedate= 27 Ocotober 2017|title= Thư tịch chữ Hán Việt Nam được khắc in ở Trung Quốc (1)|issue= 3 (106)|url= http://www.hannom.org.vn/detail.asp?param=2016&Catid=866|archiveurl= https://web.archive.org/web/20171010175518/http://www.hannom.org.vn/detail.asp?param=2016&Catid=866}}</ref> etc.) has been criticized as folk-etymological (e.g. by {{lw|vi|An Chi}}, 2016<ref name = "ac">{{cite-book|author= An Chi|year= 2016|title= Rong chơi miền chữ nghĩa|trans-title= Roving in the Land of Words and Meanings|chapter= Đề Ngạn không phải là ‘bờ sông Sài Gòn’|trans-chapter= Di'an does not mean ‘Saigon river's bank’|volume= 1|pages= 366-368}}</ref>), as: 
* (1) Garnier states that the Chinese settlement ''Tai-ngon'' or ''Tin-gan'' (i.e. {{lang|zh|堤岸}} ''Đê Ngạn'' ~ Đề Ngạn) – the Chinese name for {{w|Chợ Lớn}} – was found in 1778, yet the underlying toponym had possibly been known to Vietnamese as early as 1674, transcribed as {{zh-l|柴棍*}}, then preserved in ''{{w|Phủ biên tạp lục}}'' ({{circa2|1776|short=y}}); 
* (2) {{lang|zh|堤岸}} has variant form {{lang|zh|提岸}}, thus suggesting that both are not originals of but instead come from ''Sài Gòn''.<ref name = "ac"/>
{{col-bottom}}

