Sông Hồng.raw:
[[File:France & Colonies-1894-delta du Song-Koï.jpg|thumb|Fleuve Rouge/Song-Koï]]

===Etymology===
{{com|vi|sông|t1=river|hồng|t2=red}}, {{cal|vi|fr|[[fleuve]] [[rouge|Rouge]]|nocap=y}}. The Sinicized {{m|vi|Hồng Hà}} was also calqued from the same source.

The native/colloquial name ({{m|vi|tên Nôm}}) of the river was {{m|vi|sông Cái}}, literally "great/big river"; this word was transliterated by the French as {{m|fr|Song-Koï}} and {{m|fr|Song-Coï}}. The literary name(s) ({{m|vi|tên chữ}}) of the river was {{m|vi|珥水|tr=Nhị/Nhĩ thuỷ}} ([[:s:vi:Dụ tứ phương cần vương đối Tây Sơn hịch|textual evidence]]) and {{m|vi|珥河|tr=Nhị/Nhĩ hà}}; both the readings {{m|vi|nhĩ}} and {{m|vi|nhị}} for the first character were in use, although ''nhị'' seems to be more common. 

Since {{m|vi|紅河|tr=Hồng Hà}} was adopted late (during the colonial period), it is not clear if it ever sufficiently replaced {{m|vi|珥水|tr=Nhị/Nhĩ thuỷ}} and {{m|vi|珥河|tr=Nhị/Nhĩ hà}} as the administrative ''tên chữ''. Some of the earlier works attesting the spread of ''sông Hồng (Hà/Giang)'' as the vernacular name of the river including ''Nam Hải dị nhân liệt truyện'' (1909) and ''Việt Nam sử lược'' (1920). With the spread of ''sông Hồng'', both the native ''tên Nôm'' {{m|vi|sông Cái}} and the administrative-literary ''tên chữ'' {{m|vi|珥水|tr=Nhị/Nhĩ thuỷ}}/{{m|vi|珥河|tr=Nhị/Nhĩ hà}} were rendered obsolete.

