bánh.raw:
{{was fwotd|vi|2014|January|3}}
{{swp}}
{{swp|vi:+ (thực phẩm)}}

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}
* {{audio|vi|LL-Q9199 (vie)-Penn Zero MSSJ-bánh.wav|a=Hanoi}}

===Etymology 1===
{{vi-etym-sino|餅|flour-based food|bính|hv=n}}. Cognate with {{cog|mtq|pẻng}}, {{cog|th|แป้ง}}, {{cog|lo|ແປ້ງ}}. {{doublet|vi|pía}} (as in {{m|vi|bánh pía}}).

A [[folk etymology]] claims that ''bánh'' is a corruption of {{cog|fr|pain}}. [http://www.huffingtonpost.fr/2013/04/18/banh-mi--le-sandwich-viet_n_3110030.html] However, this is clearly false as the word is attested in early Vietnamese texts such as ''[[s:vi:Cư trần lạc đạo phú|Cư trần lạc đạo phú]]'' (居塵樂道賦, 13th century) and ''Phật thuyết đại báo phụ mẫu ân trọng kinh'' (佛說大報父母恩重經, 12th century), which were published long before French contact with Vietnam.

====Noun====
{{vi-noun|cls=[[chiếc]], [[tấm]], [[ổ]]|[[𥹘]], [[𩛄]], [[餅]], [[迸]]}}

# a broad, vaguely defined term, mostly used by collocation or intuition, for foods that come in a specific shape or solid (a flat piece, a block, a ball, a loaf, etc.) and that are often made from [[grain]] or [[legume]] [[flour]] or plain [[rice]], by size often larger than and contrasted with what is known as {{m|vi|kẹo||candy}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh''' kẹo|sweets|lit=''bánh'' and candy}}
#: {{ux|vi|Hôm nay ăn '''bánh''' thay cơm.|Today let's eat '''dumplings/pastries/bread''' instead of rice.}}
#: {{uxi|vi|bà hàng bán '''bánh'''|the lady selling '''dumplings/pastries'''}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh''' bao|'''[[baozi]]'''}}
#: {{uxi|vi|nhân '''bánh'''|'''dumpling/pastry''' filling}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh''' sinh nhật|birthday '''cake'''}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh''' mì Pháp|French bread|lit=French wheat cake}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh''' mì kẹp kem|ice cream in [[w:Bánh mì|Vietnamese baguette]]|lit=ice cream sandwich}}
#* {{see citations|vi}}
# {{lb|vi|cooking}} certain types of [[noodles]] in their raw forms
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh''' phở|[[pho]] '''noodle(s)'''}}
# a {{l|en|cake}} {{gloss|a block of any of various dense materials}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''bánh''' xà phòng ('''bánh''' xà bông)|a '''cake''' of soap}}
#* {{see citations|vi}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Not all noodles are called ''bánh'': depending on the composition and production method, some noodles are considered {{m|vi|bún}}, {{m|vi|mì}}, {{m|vi|miến}}, etc.
* Not all cakes are called ''bánh'': those made of meat are considered {{m|vi|chả||patty}}, e.g. {{m|vi|chả cá||fishcake}}, {{m|vi|chả lụa||pork roll}}, and {{m|vi|chả trứng||egg roll}}.
* ''Bánh'' is used with various classifiers, depending on the general shape of the item. A loaf of ''bánh'' is classified as {{m|vi|chiếc}} in northern Vietnam but {{m|vi|ổ}} in southern Vietnam. [https://web.archive.org/web/20151028235951/http://www.lib.washington.edu/SouthEastAsia/vsg/elist_2009/Banh%20mi.html] {{m|vi|tấm|Tấm}} is used for wafers and the like.
* ''Bánh'' is usually used as a generic word to refer to certain solid foods shaped round, flat, or puffy (e.g.: ''Cái loại '''bánh''' đó gọi là gì nhỉ? À, pi-da.'')

