bí đao.raw:
{{wp|vi:}}

===Etymology===
It is not clear what the second element is. In ''Nhật dụng thường đàm'' (日用常談, 1827) and the ''Dictionarium Anamitico-Latinum'' (1838), ''bí đao'' was spelled as {{m|vi|[[秘]][[刀]]}} and {{m|vi|[[費]][[刀]]}} respectively. In both cases, the second element is spelled with {{ltc-l|刀|blade}} ([[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: {{m|vi|đao}}), which makes little sense if taken literally ("knife gourd" or "sword gourd"), so it is most likely a phonogram used simply for phonetic value (just like the adjacent phonograms {{ltc-l|秘}} and {{ltc-l|費|id=2}} used to write {{m|vi|bí||gourd}}).

Considering that winter melon/wax gourd is commonly used to make sweets (''Đại Nam quấc âm tự vị'' (1895) mentions {{m|vi|[[mứt]] bí||candied winter melon}}), the second element could be related to {{cog|aem|-}} ''atɛːw'', {{cog|pac|atao}}, {{cog|bdq|kơtao}}, all means "sugarcane"; if so, probably developed already into "sugary sweet" when this compound was coined, then became obscure. 
<!-- I've also thought of the idea that it is borrowed a form of 冬 that had lost the nasal coda, but such lects with that feature are few and far from the geographic range of Vietnamese. -->

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

