===Etymology===
{{vi-etym-sino|高等}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

===Adjective===
{{vi-adj}}

# related to {{l|en|[[junior college]]/[[community college]]}}
#: {{ux|vi|trường cao đẳng|a junior/community college}}
#: {{ux|vi|bằng cao đẳng|an associate('s) degree}}
#: {{ux|vi|học cao đẳng|to go to junior/community college; to study an associate program}}
# {{lb|vi|of an organism|or|species}} [[advanced]] or [[complex]]

====Usage notes====
In Vietnam, there is an arbitrary distinction between the English words {{m|en|university}} and {{m|en|college}} in the English names of Vietnamese institutions. {{m|en|university|University}} is used of four-year bachelor's institutions, and these include both collective ones and members of them. For example, ''Đại học Quốc gia Thành phố Hồ Chí Minh'' is called the "Vietnam National University, Ho Chi Minh City," but one of its members, ''Đại học Công nghệ Thông tin'', is equally called "University of Information Technology"; compare this to the use of {{m|vi|university}} and {{m|en|college}} in English, as in the {{m|en||University of Oxford}} and {{m|en||Worcester College}}. On the other hand, {{m|en|college}} in Vietnamese contexts is used only of two-year associate's institutions, despite its near synonymy to {{m|en|university}} in proper English, especially American English. This contrived distinction often gives Vietnamese learners of English a false impression of how the English words are to be used, as respective direct translations of the Vietnamese words {{m|vi|đại học}} and {{m|vi|cao đẳng}}.

