chồng.raw:
===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}
* {{audio|vi|LL-Q9199 (vie)-Jessica Nguyen (Pamputt)-chồng.wav|a=Ho Chi Minh City}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|vi|mkh-vie-pro|*p-ʄoːŋ ~ *ɟoːŋ}}. Cognate with {{cog|mra|-}} ''ɟioŋ'' ("father"), {{cog|kjg|-}} ''joŋ'' ("man"). 

This word originally just meant "man, male", as with North Central {{m|vi|gấy}}/{{m|vi|cấy||wife}}, originally just meant "woman, female".

Attested in ''Annan Jishi'' (安南即事, 13th century) as {{ltc-l|重|id=1}} (modern [[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: {{m|vi|trùng}}). Attested as Northern {{cog|mkh-mvi|chà{{mkh-mvi-apex|o}}}} in the ''Dictionarium Annamiticum Lusitanum et Latinum'' (1651), a dictionary chiefly based on the Northern dialects. The modern Central forms descended from unattested Middle Vietnamese dialects, which further derived from unattested non-Northern Old Vietnamese dialects.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|vi|nhôông|nhông|q1=North Central Vietnam}}
* {{alt|vi|dôông|dông|q1=North Central Vietnam}}
* {{alt|vi|dôn|q1=Huế|pos=same as ''dông'' with ‹-ng› vs. ‹-n› merger}}
* {{alt|vi|ck|q1=texting, abbreviation}}

====Noun====
{{vi-noun|[[重]], [[𫯰]], [[𫯳]]}}

# [[husband]]
#: {{uxi|vi|lấy '''chồng'''|{{q|of a woman}} to get married}}
#: {{uxi|vi|bố/mẹ '''chồng'''|husband's father/mother}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In {{m|vi|[[vợ]] chồng||wife and husband}}, {{m|vi|[[cô]] [[chú]]||paternal aunt and her husband}}, {{m|vi|cô [[cậu]]||you girls and boys}}, {{m|vi|[[dì]] [[dượng]]||maternal aunt and her husband; stepmother and stepfather}}, the words for females always come first. In most other phrases, the words for males usually come first, except in certain poetic contexts (e.g. {{m|vi|[[mẹ]] [[cha]]||mum and dad}} as opposed to the usual {{m|vi||cha mẹ|dad and mum}}).

=====See also=====
{{col|vi|vợ chồng|nhà chồng}}

=====Further reading=====
* [https://www.chunom.org/pages/2BBF3/ chunom.org]

===Etymology 2===
{{vi-etym-sino|重||hv=n|trùng}}.

