cà phê.raw:
{{swp|vi:}}

===Etymology===
{{dercat|vi|fr|it|ota|ar}}
{{rfe|vi|It is attested as ''cà phe'' in the ''Dictionarium Anamitico-Latinum'' (1838), sourced from a dictionary by Pigneau (1773). I'm not sure how much that would affect the etymologizing below, which is based on sources and works quite a bit later.}}
Adapted from an earlier form {{m|vi|trà phê||[[coffee]]}} (Martini, 1958; Michaud ''et al.'', 2015), as attested in scholar {{w|Pétrus Ky}}'s ''Cours d'annamite parlé'' [''A course in spoken Annamese''] (1894). This is a {{psm|vi|fr|café||coffee|nocap=1}}, matching the first syllable with the existing Sino-Vietnamese word {{m|vi|trà||[[tea]]}}; literally, this name could be interpreted as: “a kind of tea with the ''phê'' attribute” (whatever that attribute may mean).

Naming coffee as a type of tea acclimatises the new beverage, introducing it into a set that already included {{m|vi|trà tàu||Chinese tea (black tea)}}, {{m|vi|trà Huế||Annamese tea (green tea)}}, {{m|vi|trà hoa||camellia tea}}, {{m|vi|trà rừng||three-seeded mercury (herbal) tea}}, and so on (Martini, 1958; Michaud ''et al.'', 2015). An example is found in ''Cours d'annamite parlé'':
: {{ux|vi|anh uống '''trà-phe''' hay-là trà-tàu|Do you drink '''coffee''' [the ''phe'' tea] or tea [the Chinese tea]?}}

Later on, the form ''trà phê'' underwent re-foreignisation, influenced by French-speaking Vietnamese who pronounce French ''café'' as ‹''ca phê''› (Michaud ''et al.'', 2015). A hybrid form of ''cà phê'' emerged as a consequence, retaining the original {{IPAchar|/k/}} in the donor language and the tone of ''trà'', and this form eventually became the standard (Nguyễn, 1999).

An alternative theory for the unexpected tone on ''cà'' was put forth by Emeneau (1951: 4, 158), who tentatively proposed that it was due to association with {{m|vi|cà||[[eggplant]]}}, in a fashion similar to {{m|vi|cà rốt||[[carrot]]}}. However, this is now deemed to be very unlikely.

Compare {{m|vi|phê||high; intoxicated}}, as well as {{cog|yue|咖啡|tr=gaa3 fe1|pos=also with a lower tone on the first syllable}}.

