cái.raw:
===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}
* {{audio|vi|LL-Q9199 (vie)-Penn Zero MSSJ-cái.wav|a=Hanoi}}

===Etymology 1===
{{rfe|vi|Compare {{cog|pac|ki||that, which}}, {{cog|sza|-}} ''ke'' ("that"); if these are actual cognates, it's possible that this etymon already developed into a topicalizer/focus marker at a pre-Common Viet-Muong stage, which might explain why it's used to mark both animate and inanimate nouns in Muong and (pre-modern) Vietnamese.}}
Cognate with {{cog|mtq|-}} Bi {{m|mtq|cảy}}, {{cog|tou|-}} [Cuối Chăm] ''keː³'', {{cog|scb|-}} [Mày] ''kɛ⁴'' (Babaev & Samarina, 2018), {{cog|scb|-}} [Rục] ''kɛ⁴'' (Nguyễn Văn Lợi, 1993).

Often linked with {{ncog|zh|個||one thing; ''classifier''}} (for example, in Alves (2007)); however, as seen with the cognates in the more conservative languages, the earlier vowel can be ascertained to be a monophthongal non-low front vowel, which makes connection with the Chinese term very much doubtful, although semantic influence is still possible.

In Middle Vietnamese, this classifier is also attested to be used for certain animals (e.g. ''cái'' {{m|vi|kiến||an ant}}, ''cái'' {{m|vi|ba ba|baba|a soft-shell turtle}}). However, in Modern Vietnamese, this function has been completely taken over by the general animate classifier {{m|vi|con}}. Although sometimes characterized as historically being used for animals that are on the smaller size, plenty of small animals took ''con'' instead.

In Muong dialects, this usage can also be seen. For example, {{cog|mtq|-}} Bi has {{m|mtq|[[cảy]] [[lòi]]||a wild boar}}, {{m|mtq|[[cảy]] [[ca]]||a chicken}}.

====Classifier====
{{vi-cls|[[丐]], [[𫡔]], [[個]]}}

# {{n-g|Indicates an [[inanimate]], [[tangible]] thing}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Cái''' răng '''cái''' tóc là góc con người.|Teeth and [head]hair are [[part and parcel]] of the human figure ~ Teeth and [head]hair tell how good one looks.}}
# {{lb|vi|obsolete}} {{n-g|Indicates animals}}
#* {{quote-book|vi|title=vi:里巷歌謠<tr:Lý hạng ca dao><t:Folk Ballads from the Hamlets and Alleys>|url=https://lib.nomfoundation.org/collection/1/volume/26/page/30|page=30a|passage='''丐'''𪂲'''丐'''𪅥'''丐'''𪆯<br>𫳵𡮠踸𪽣穭翁唉𪂲|tr='''Cái''' cò, '''cái''' vạc, '''cái''' nông,<br>Sao mày giẫm ruộng lúa ông hỡi cò?|t=The stork, the heron, the pelican;<br>Why troddest thou on my paddy rice, oh stork?}}
# {{lb|vi|colloquial}} {{n-g|Precedes another classifier (any one but “cái” itself), effectively acting as a focus marker, sometimes conveying a connotation of deprecation, especially if persons are referred to.}}
#: {{ux|vi|Cầm '''cái''' con dao này đưa cho mẹ.|Take this knife and hand it to your mom.}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Cái''' thằng đần thối này!|You stinkin' moron!}}

=====See also=====
* {{l|vi|chiếc}}; {{l|vi|con}}

====Noun====
{{vi-noun|[[丐]], [[𫡔]], [[個]]}}

# {{lb|vi|followed by verb}} [[utensil]] for
#: {{syn|vi|đồ}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''cái''' đánh trứng|a whisk|lit='''utensil for''' whisking eggs}}
# {{lb|vi|followed by adjective}} that which is
#: {{uxi|vi|'''cái''' đẹp|beauty|lit='''that which is''' beautiful}}
#: {{uxi|vi|'''cái''' cao cả|the sublime|lit='''that which is''' elevated}}
# {{lb|vi|followed by adverb}} way of working, way of doing (something)
#: {{par|vi|sự}}
#* {{RQ:Tran Trong Kim Nho giao|pages=11|volume=I|chapter=Tựa|url=https://vi.wikisource.org/wiki/Nho_gi%C3%A1o/Quy%E1%BB%83n_I/T%E1%BB%B1a|passage=Ước-ao rằng '''cái''' công-phu này không đến nỗi bỏ uổng vậy.|translation=We only hope that this painstaking '''work''' will not be for nothing.}}
# the [[solid]] [[bit]]s (of a [[broth]])
#: {{ux|vi|Canh này thì chỉ có nước là ngon, còn '''cái''' thì dở ẹc.|Only the liquid part of this broth tastes good, the '''solid bits''' suck.}}

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|vi
|cái gì
|cái này
|lạ nước lạ cái
}}

=====See also=====
* {{vi-l|nước||{{l|en|liquid}}; liquid part of a broth}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|vi|mkh-vie-pro|*-keːʔ||[[woman]]; [[female]]}}. Compare {{m|vi|gái}}.

Attested in the title of {{w|Phùng Hưng}}, {{ltc-l|布蓋大王|id=1, 1, 1, 1}} ([[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: {{m|vi|[[bố|Bố]] [[cái|Cái]] [[Đại Vương|Đại Vương]]}}), with the first two characters rendering words ancestral to modern {{m|vi|vua||monarch}} and ''cái'' ("great, main"), equivalent to the Chinese {{l|zh|大王}} next to it, i.e. the title is the phrase "great king/monarch" written in two languages.<ref>{{cite-thesis|en|author=John Duong Phan|year=2013|url=https://ecommons.cornell.edu/items/82422b28-3e0a-47b2-803b-38e6a21203c5|title=Lacquered words: The evolution of Vietnamese under Sinitic influences from the 1{{sup|st}} century BCE through the 17{{sup|th}} century CE|pages=248; 373–374|publisher={{w|Cornell University}}}}</ref>

For the "host" sense, compare semantically {{ncog|ja|親|tr=oya|t=parent, dealer, banker, etc.}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alt|vi|cấy|q=North Central Vietnam}}

====Noun====
{{vi-noun|[[丐]], [[𡛔]]}}

