cám ơn.raw:
{{phrasebook|vi}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{q|originally Northern Vietnam, now prescriptive}} {{l|vi|cảm ơn}}

===Etymology===
Nativisation of [[Chinese]] [[感恩]] (modern [[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: [[cảm ân]]). First attested in the 17th century as {{m|vi|cám ơn}} and the only form to exist in old dictionaries.

In 1895, when the first volume of ''Đại Nam quấc âm tự vị'' was published by ''Imprimerie Rey, Curiol & Cie'' printer in Saigon, only {{m|vi|cám ơn}} was attested.

In 1931, ''Việt Nam tự điển'' (1931) by ''Hội Khai trí Tiến đức'' in Hanoi was the first dictionary to record the form {{m|vi|cảm ân}}, which might have resulted from a learned reinterpretation of the Chinese characters according to their regular Sino-Vietnamese readings. During this period, {{m|vi|cảm ơn}} was also attested elsewhere, possibly resulting from the replacement of the first syllable of {{m|vi|cám ơn}} by analogy with {{m|vi|cảm ân}}.

In 1967, ''Từ điển tiếng Việt'' (1967) by ''Văn Tân,'' published in North Vietnam, became the first dictionary to record the form {{m|vi|cảm ơn}}. In South Vietnam, however, {{m|vi|cám ơn}} remained the standard form in official publications.

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

===Verb===
{{vi-verb|[[敢恩]], [[感恩]]}}

# to [[thank]]
#: {{ux|vi|(Cháu/Con) cám ơn cô.|(I'd like to) thank you.}}
#: {{ux|vi|Cám ơn chú đi con.|Go ahead and thank him.}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Cám ơn''' các anh '''đã''' tới đây.|Thank you all for coming.}}
#: {{ux|vi|(Con) '''cám ơn''' mẹ '''đã''' sinh thành và nuôi nấng con.|Mother, thank you for bearing and rearing me.}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Cám ơn vì''' bức thư của em.|Thank you for your letter.}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Cám ơn''' (rất) nhiều (nhé/nha).|Many thanks. / Thank you very much. / Merci beaucoup.}}
#: {{ux|vi|Chân thành '''cám ơn'''.|I'd like to express my deep gratitude.}}
#: {{ux|vi|'''Xin cám ơn''' mọi người.|I'd like to thank everyone.}}
#* {{quote-book|vi|year=2012|author=w:Joe Ruelle|title=Ngược chiều vun vút|trans-title=Whooshing toward the Other Way|url=https://books.google.com.vn/books?id=IlpOMwEACAAJ|text=Anh vẫn nhớ lần đầu tiên lên sân khấu tặng hoa cho em. Em cười duyên, nói “cảm ơn”. Ôi, lúc đó em không thể hiểu anh xúc động như thế nào! Anh xem tất cả các clip phỏng vấn của em trên truyền hình, xem đi xem lại, và anh thấy em luôn chỉ nói “'''cám ơn'''” – tức chữ “cám” có dấu sắc. Nhưng chắc chắn, một trăm phần trăm, lúc mình gặp nhau trên sân khấu ngày hôm đó em đã nói “cảm ơn” với anh, chữ “cảm” có dấu hỏi. […] Điều em muốn nói (nhưng không thể nói được trước đám đông) là em cảm thấy đặc biệt ấn tượng với anh, rất muốn biết thêm về anh, muốn “hỏi” anh rất nhiều chuyện.|t=I still remember the first time I went onstage and gave you flowers. You smiled elegantly, and you said “cảm ơn”. Oh, you wouldn’t have known how touched I was then! I’d watched all of your interviews on TV, all over and over again, and I’d noticed you’d always said “'''cám ơn'''” – the “cám” with the ''sắc'' tone. But I’m one hundred percent sure that, at the moment we met onstage that day, you said “cảm ơn” to me, with the “cảm” with the ''hỏi'' tone. […] What you wanted to profess (but couldn’t in front of the whole crowd) was that you felt especially impressed by me, that you wanted to know a lot more about me, that you wanted to “hỏi” (ask) me about a lot of things.|chapter=Chuyện tôi, Mỹ Tâm và nước mắm Phú Quốc|trans-chapter=The Story about Me, Mỹ Tâm and Phú Quốc Fish Sauce|brackets=on}}

====Usage notes====
* Similar to {{m|vi|chào}}, {{m|vi|chúc}} and {{m|vi|xin lỗi}}, a subject is not needed, if you're the one who's doing the thanking. Even young children can get away without a subject.
* Unlike {{m|vi|chào}} and {{m|vi|chúc}}, only compatible with {{m|vi|xin}}, not {{m|vi|kính}}. Thus, ''xin cám ơn'', not *''kính cám ơn''.
* The difference between {{m|vi||cám ơn}} and {{m|vi||cảm ơn}} is not all clear-cut, and it's largely based on personal preference or gut feelings. Some may insist that {{m|vi||cám ơn}} is more colloquial, others may insist {{m|vi||cảm ơn}} is somehow more "correct", others still may insist that the former feels more southern and the latter more northern.
* In quick speech, the tone of {{m|vi|cám}} may be weakly enunciated and the glottal stop of {{m|vi|ơn}} may be dropped, yielding {{IPAfont|[kaməːn]}} with a shorter {{m|vi|sắc}} tone.

