{{vi-etym-sino|分|to divide|子|child; small thing}}, from {{bor|vi|ja|分子|tr=bunshi}}, irregularly adapted from {{der|vi|ltc|分子|tr=pjun tsiX||numerator|lit=division child}} (a regular form of the Japanese word would be {{m|ja|分子|tr=*funshi}}). As a mathematical term, it is opposed to {{m|ltc|分母||tr=pjun muwX|denominator|lit=division mother}}, and both were mentioned in ''{{w|The Nine Chapters on the Mathematical Art}}''. Later extended to physics in the 20th century. The voiceless obstruent {{IPAchar|/p/}} in the {{ncog|ltc|分|tr=pjun||to divide}} resulted in the {{m|vi|ngang}} tone, as opposed to {{m|vi|phần tử}}. {{doublet|vi|phần tử}}. Cognate with {{cog|cmn|分子|tr=fēnzǐ}} and {{cog|yue|分子|tr=fan1 zi2}}.

