tươm.raw:
===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}

===Etymology 1===
Ferlus suggested that {{inh|vi|mkh-pro|*saːm||to ooze, to bleed}} (whence {{inh|vi|mkh-vie-pro|*saːm}} > ''tươm'') is the verbal base from which the word ‘blood’ in the Austroasiatic languages is derived, except for Vietnamese and for some Vietic languages in which it was replaced by ‘sap' (Viet. {{m|vi|máu}}). From PMK ''*saːm'', {{cog|mkh-vie-pro|*ʔa-saːmʔ}} ~ {{m|mkh-vie-pro|*p-saːmʔ||blood}}, {{cog|mkh-ban-pro|*bhaːm}} (> {{cog|bdq|pham}}), {{cog|mkh-kat-pro|*ʔahaam}} (> {{cog|pac|aham}}), Proto-Khasic *snaam (>{{cog|kha|snam}}/{{m|kha|'nam}}), {{cog|km|ឈាម}}, {{cog|mnw|ဆီ|tr=c'him}} and {{cog|caq|ma-hām}} were derived, through different affixed forms.[http://sealang.net/sala/archives/pdf8/ferlus2008le.pdf]

Shorto reconstructed {{cog|mkh-pro|*ɟhaam|*ɟhaam ~ *ɟhiim|blood}}, based on the Khmer and Mon forms.

