[[w:Vietnamese pronouns|Vietnamese pronouns]] usually consist of very specific kinship terms that depend on gender, age, and social status, and traditionally the language lacks a generic second person pronoun equivalent to "you". In most formal settings, a speaker usually addresses an audience as "{{m|vi|quý vị}}". However, in a Christian setting where an English speaker might say "{{m|en|brothers and sisters}}", "{{m|vi|ông bà anh chị em}}" is used, especially if much older people may be present. If the audience consists of people of roughly the same age as the speaker or younger, "{{m|vi|anh chị em}}" is used instead.

====Synonyms====
* {{vi-l|ông bà và anh chị em}}
* {{vi-l|quý khách}}
* {{vi-l|quý vị}}

====Related terms====
* {{vi-l|anh chị em}}

ông kẹ.raw:
===Etymology===
{{rfe|vi|The practice of having a nickname for addressing in everyday life is still very common nowadays. Usually these nicknames are just words for normal everyday things (animals, fruits, etc.), some with opaque meanings (Tèo, etc.), or "lowly" things. This Kẹ might belong to the same category as Tèo.}}
Per {{w|Huỳnh Tịnh Của|Huình Tịnh Của}} (1895), this was original a nickname of ''Nguyễn Văn Trị'', a retainer of {{w|Gia Long}}, said to be an honest but short-tempered man.
{{quote-book|vi|year=1895|author={{w|Huỳnh Tịnh Của|Huình Tịnh Của}}|title=Đại Nam Quấc Âm Tự Vị|text=<blockquote>偈 '''Kẹ'''. n.<br/>'''Ông''' ―. Tưởng là ông Trị, là người có công dày với đức Cao-hoàng, đến khi phục quấc, người ban cho một cái bài miễn tử; bổn tánh ông ấy thật thà, hay khí bất bình, ai nấy đều sợ, cho nên có tiếng nhát con nít rằng: ý hà '''ông kẹ'''! v. v.</blockquote>|t=<blockquote>偈 '''Kẹ'''. n.<br/>'''Ông''' ―. Thought to be Sir Trị, a loyal retainer of Emperor {{w|Gia Long|Cao-hoàng}}; once the country was restored, His Majesty granted him immunity privileges; he was a man of honest character, but did not have a cool head; commoners were terrified him, and they said things like "Watch out for '''Old Man Kẹ'''!", etc. when they wanted to scare their children.</blockquote>}}

Unfortunately, this early account does not state neither the meaning nor origin of this nickname.

