From {{inh|vi|mkh-vie-pro|*l-taːʔ||rock}}. Cognate with {{cog|aem|-}} ''atæːˀ''. Attested in ''Phật thuyết đại báo phụ mẫu ân trọng kinh'' (佛說大報父母恩重經) as {{m|vi|𪜀打}}, the simplified composition of {{ltc-l|羅}} + {{ltc-l|打|id=1}} (modern [[Sino-Vietnamese|SV]]: {{m|vi|la}} {{m|vi|đả}}).

Schuessler (2007: p. 462)<ref>{{cite-book|last= Schuessler|first= Axel|year= 2007|title= ABC Etymological Dictionary of Old Chinese|location= Honolulu|publisher= University of Hawaii Press|isbn=978-0-8248-2975-9|url= https://starlingdb.org/Texts/Students/Schuessler,%20Axel/ABC%20Etymological%20Dictionary%20of%20Old%20Chinese%20(2007).pdf}}.</ref> proposes that this Vietic word and Khmer {{m|km|ដា}} (which is likely borrowed from Vietnamese or another Vietic language instead of being inherited from a common source) might be related to {{ncog|zh|-}} {{och-l|石|tr={{IPAfont|*dAk}}}} (B-S) (SV: {{m|vi|thạch}}). However, this is unlikely due to both:
* the mismatch between final *-k and *-ʔ, which requires Schuessler to assume that Chinese added "the familiar final ''-k''" after foreign *-ʔ, resulting in *-ʔ-k > *-k (for an analogy, {{ncog|och|-}} {{och-l|汝|tr=*naʔ}} > {{och-l|若|tr=*nak}}), therefore reducing his proposal's [[parsimony]];
* that so far only nasal pre-initials, not *{{IPAfont|-l-}}, are known to have a voicing effect on Old Chinese voiceless initials (see for an example Baxter & Sagart, 2014: p. 81-83,<ref>{{cite-book|author=[[w:William H. Baxter|Baxter, William H.]]; [[w:Laurent Sagart|Sagart, Laurent]]|year=2014|title=Old Chinese: A New Reconstruction|location=New York|publisher=Oxford University Press}}</ref> among others); so Proto-Vietic {{IPAfont|*l-t-}} would unlikely become Old Chinese {{IPAfont|*d-}}.
<!-- Many of Schuessler's proposed connections between AA and Sinitic seem very dubious, this is one of them.-->

