đứa.raw:
===Etymology===
From {{inh|vi|mkh-vie-pro|*taːʔ}}, from {{inh|vi|aav-pro|*taːʔ||grandfather}}. Cognate with {{cog|mtq|-}} Bi {{m|mtq|tửa||man, male; inferior one}}, {{cog|aem|-}} ''tæːˀ'' ("elder brother"), {{cog|thm|-}} ''tâ̰ː'' (Suwilai, 2000; the tonal correspondence to Vietnamese is regular). The term is attested with the meaning "elderly man" in Thavung, "elder brother" in Arem, "man, male" in Muong and "inferior one, kiddie" in Vietnamese, showing the gradual semantic development. Note that {{cog|scb|-}} [Rục] ''taː¹'' ("maternal grandfather") with tone A1 is a Tai loan (cf. {{cog|tai-pro|*taːᴬ}}, whence {{cog|th|ตา}}) that while undoubtedly related to the Vietic word (and other Austroasiatic words below) is not a reflex of Proto-Vietic ''*taːʔ'', where the final glottal stop developed regularly to the tone of the Vietnamese, Muong and Thavung words, or, in the case of Arem, preserved. <!-- There is also the doublet tả "grandfather" in Mường. Not sure if this is simply variants like the case of cái/gái or some dialect mixing is involved.-->

Further cognates included {{cog|km|តា}}, {{cog|kjg|-}} ''taʔ'' ("grandfather"), {{cog|ply|-}} ''ta³¹'' ("grandfather"), {{cog|mnw|တ||father}}, {{cog|tea|taak||sir, grandfather}}. Compare the development seen in {{ncog|mtq|dã||mother-in-law, grandmother}} and Maleng Bro ''jaː³'' ("father's elder brother's wife") (from {{ncog|mkh-vie-pro|*-jaːʔ}}, from {{ncog|mkh-pro|*jaʔ||grandmother}}).

===Pronunciation===
{{vi-IPA}}
* {{audio|vi|LL-Q9199 (vie)-Penn Zero MSSJ-đứa.wav|a=Hanoi}}

===Classifier===
{{vi-cls|[[丁]], [[仃]], [[侈]], [[打]], [[𠁂]], [[𠀲]], [[𫯍]], [[矴]], [[𥒥]], [[𱍢]]}}

# ''indicates [[young]] person, [[child]], or an [[inferior]] person''
#* {{RQ:Nguyen Ngoc Tu CDBT|chapter=Nhà cổ|passage=Hai '''đứa''' tôi ngồi đâu đó (chỗ mà ai cũng nhìn thấy) nói chuyện chơi, có lúc, chẳng cần nói gì.
|translation=The two of us were sitting somewhere (a place where everyone could see us) chatting, sometimes not needing to say anything.}}

====Usage notes====
* While never used to refer to a person that one thinks is their superior, ''đứa'' is not very offensive and can easily used by one to refer to themselves or address their peers:
*: {{ux|vi|Hay '''bốn đứa''' mình đâu ăn đi?|Why don't '''us 4''' go eat somewhere?}}

