ấy.raw:
===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|vi|í}}
* {{alt|vi|ý}}

===Etymology===
{{rfe|vi}}

Diachronically, this is probably a Northern Vietnamese innovation occured some time between the stages of Proto-Vietnamese and attested Northern Old Vietnamese. It is amply attested in the oldest Nôm texts (which are all in Northern Old Vietnamese or its offshoot(s)), yet has no cognate in the Muong lects, and usage of this morpheme by Central and Southern speakers clearly indicates borrowing. It also does not neatly fit into Proto-Vietnamese demonstrative paradigm with its zero-initial.

{{box-top|Development of distal demonstratives in Vietnamese}}
The situation can probably be hypothesized as:
{| class="wikitable"
|-
! !! Proto-Vietnamese !! Old Vietnamese !! Modern Vietnamese
|-
| Northern dialects || rowspan = "3" | Full paradigm of demonstratives. (The ancestral forms of) ''ấy'' and {{m|vi|tê}} not yet innovated. Ancestral forms of {{m|vi|đấy}} (''đ-''series, distal 1), {{m|vi|đó}} (''đ-''series, distal 3), {{m|vi|nấy}} (''n-''series, distal 1), {{m|vi|nớ}} (''n-''series, distal 2), {{m|vi|nọ}} (''n-''series, distal 3) in full use. || Northern Old Vietnamese innovated the ancestral form of ''ấy'', probably from an irregular loss of the alveolar stop onset of the corresponding form of {{m|vi|đấy}}. Ancestral forms of {{m|vi|đó}} and {{m|vi|nớ}} fell into disuse. || Inherited {{m|vi|đấy}} from Proto-Vietnamese and ''ấy'' from Northern Old Vietnamese. Borrowed {{m|vi|đó}} from Southern dialects, with limited usage.<sup>1</sup> The rest of the paradigm became obsolete, literary, or restricted to idioms.
|-
| Central dialects || rowspan = "2" | Heterogeneous unattested Old Vietnamese dialects ancestral to modern Central and Southern dialects. Ancestral form of {{m|vi|đấy}} likely fell into disuse, with ancestral forms of {{m|vi|nớ}} and {{m|vi|đó}} filling its place. || Inherited {{m|vi|nớ}} from Proto-Vietnamese, innovated {{m|vi|tê}} at unknown stage. Borrowed {{m|vi|đấy}} and ''ấy'' from Northern dialects and {{m|vi|đó}} from Southern dialects, with limited usage. The rest of the paradigm became obsolete, literary, or restricted to idioms.
|-
| Southern dialects || Inherited {{m|vi|đó}} from Proto-Vietnamese. Borrowed {{m|vi|đấy}} and ''ấy'' from Northern dialects, with limited usage. The rest of the paradigm became obsolete, literary, or restricted to idioms.
|-
| colspan="4" style="text-align:left; font-size:smaller;" | <sup>1</sup> There is attestation of ''đó'' (spelled {{m|vi|妬}}) in ''Hồng Đức quốc âm thi tập'' (洪德國音詩集, late 15th century), so it was probably borrowed already by then (or it was a poem composed by a speaker of a non-Northern dialect, but attributed to {{w|Lê Thánh Tông}}). Earlier texts, all in Northern Vietnamese, like ''Phật thuyết đại báo phụ mẫu ân trọng kinh'' (佛說大報父母恩重經, ''circa'' 12th century), ''Cư trần lạc đạo phú'' (居塵樂道賦, 13th century), or ''Quốc âm thi tập'' (國音詩集, early 15th century) only show {{m|vi|đấy}} and ''ấy''.
